#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Last check before something becomes irreversible. Runs for every repo under
# the fleet root, from a clone or a worktree, for the owner and for every agent.
#
# `scripts/install-hooks.py` installs a four-line shim at the `core.hooksPath`
# git actually reads and points it here; this file is the implementation and the
# only copy to edit. `install-hooks.py --check` reports drift.
#
# Why a pre-push hook exists when there is already a ten-check gate: the gate
# runs when a supervisor folds, which in several paths is AFTER a push. Two
# things escaped to `origin/main` on 2026-09-07 and were found by the gate
# afterwards:
#
#   * A client product name, a client repo name and the workspace path holding
#     both, in `embarch-fleet/hardware.toml`. Scrubbed forward six minutes
#     later, but the strings are in that commit's tree for good. It was the
#     SECOND instance: a supervisor log entry put a client name on
#     `origin/main` for 21 hours on 2026-09-05, removed by `cab9903`.
#   * Two files sharing `tasks/ui/016`, issued twenty minutes apart by a leg
#     and by the owner, each reading the same "next free" before either
#     committed.
#
# The two are treated differently ON PURPOSE, and the asymmetry is the design:
# a name in pushed history cannot be taken back without rewriting every SHA
# after it, so that BLOCKS. A duplicate task number is caught by the gate and
# fixed with a `git mv`, so that WARNS. A hook that blocks on a recoverable
# problem can wedge a leg mid-fold, which is a worse failure than the one it
# would prevent.
set -uo pipefail

# --- BLOCKING: credentials -------------------------------------------------
# Preserved verbatim from the hand-written `~/.git-hooks-personal/pre-push`
# this file took over on 2026-09-07, and deliberately FIRST: it is the guard
# that keeps a personal repo from being pushed with work credentials, and it
# was previously unversioned -- the same "the mechanism lives outside every
# repo" shape `usage-budget.py`'s `check_feeder` exists to detect.
remote_url="${2-}"
if [[ "$remote_url" == git@github.com:* ]] || [[ "$remote_url" == https://github.com/* ]]; then
  echo "error: pushing to '$remote_url' would authenticate with your default (work) credentials." >&2
  echo "Use the personal host alias instead, e.g. git@github.com-personal:<user>/<repo>.git" >&2
  exit 1
fi

repo_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
fleet_scripts="__FLEET_SCRIPTS__"
fleet_root="__FLEET_ROOT__"

# Everything below is fleet-specific. The credential guard above is not, so it
# runs first and unconditionally.
case "$repo_root/" in
  "$fleet_root"/*) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

fail=0

# --- BLOCKING: client names ------------------------------------------------
if [ -f "$fleet_scripts/check-client-names.py" ]; then
  if ! out=$(python3 "$fleet_scripts/check-client-names.py" --repo "$repo_root" 2>&1); then
    printf '%s\n' "$out"
    cat <<'MSG'

PUSH REFUSED: a denylisted client name is in the tree you are about to push.

This is not recoverable by a later commit. A name in pushed history stays in
that commit's tree, and taking it out means rewriting every SHA after it.

Fix it here, before the push:
  1. The report above names the file and line, and an entry NUMBER rather than
     the name -- read the number in .fleet/client-names. Do not paste the name
     into a commit message, a log entry or an agent transcript; that is the
     leak, not the fix.
  2. Move the value to a machine-local file outside every repo, next to the
     denylist itself, which is kept there for exactly this reason.
     `embarch-fleet/hardware.toml`'s header is the worked example.
  3. Re-run the check, then push.

MSG
    fail=1
  fi
fi

# --- ADVISORY: task numbers ------------------------------------------------
if [ -f "$repo_root/scripts/check-task-numbers.py" ]; then
  if ! out=$(python3 "$repo_root/scripts/check-task-numbers.py" 2>&1); then
    printf '%s\n' "$out"
    cat <<'MSG'

WARNING, NOT REFUSED: a task number is duplicated or reissued in what you are
about to push. The push goes ahead, because this is recoverable and a hook that
blocks a fold is worse than the collision.

Fix it now rather than leaving it on main -- `check-docs.py` is RED until you
do, and that blocks the next fold:
  scripts/check-task-numbers.py --next <scope>    # a number safe to issue
  git mv tasks/<scope>/<old> tasks/<scope>/<new>  # then fix the H1 heading

The number that LOST the race is the one that moves: history records whichever
slug landed first, and `tasks/README.md` says a number is never reused.

MSG
  fi
fi

exit $fail
